      body:
        "**Context** means the world in which a text was written and first watched. You do not need to write a history essay, but a sentence of well-chosen context can unlock a whole speech.\n\n**When?** The Tempest was written around **1610–11**. Its first recorded performance was at court, in front of **King James I**, on 1 November 1611. It is usually described as the **last play Shakespeare wrote on his own** (later plays such as *Henry VIII* were co-written). When the **First Folio** collection of his plays was printed in 1623, The Tempest was placed first.\n\n**The Sea Venture.** In 1609 a ship called the *Sea Venture*, sailing to the new English colony at **Jamestown, Virginia**, was caught in a violent storm and wrecked on **Bermuda**. Everyone was feared drowned — but the passengers survived on the island, built new boats and reached Virginia almost a year later. Letters describing the wreck, such as William Strachey's account, circulated in London, and many scholars think Shakespeare read one. Ariel even mentions 'the still-vex'd Bermoothes' (the stormy Bermudas).\n\n**Colonisation.** In Shakespeare's time Europeans were sailing to the Americas and claiming lands that were already lived in. Audiences were fascinated by travellers' tales of 'new worlds' and the people found there. This is why many modern readers see Prospero's arrival on Caliban's island as a story of **colonialism** — one people taking control of another people's land.\n\n**Montaigne.** The French writer Michel de Montaigne's essay **'Of Cannibals'** (translated into English in 1603) argued that so-called 'savages' were in many ways more natural and decent than 'civilised' Europeans. Gonzalo's dream of a perfect island society in Act 2 Scene 1 borrows closely from it. Many critics also notice that the name **Caliban** is almost an anagram of 'canibal'.\n\n**A farewell?** Because Prospero gives up his magic, breaks his staff and asks the audience to set him free, some readers see him as a portrait of Shakespeare saying goodbye to the theatre. This is an **interpretation**, not a fact — present it as 'some critics argue…'.\n\n- c.1610–11; performed at court for James I in 1611\n- The 1609 Sea Venture wreck off Bermuda\n- Early colonisation of the Americas\n- Montaigne's 'Of Cannibals'\n- Shakespeare's last solo-authored play",
