      id: "caliban",
      heading: "Caliban: 'This island's mine'",
      body:
        "**Caliban** is the son of the witch **Sycorax**, who was banished from Algiers to the island. After she died he lived there alone — until Prospero arrived. He is one of Shakespeare's most argued-about characters.\n\n**The case for sympathy.** In Act 1 Scene 2, Caliban claims ownership: 'This island's mine, by Sycorax my mother, / Which thou tak'st from me.' The **monosyllables** of 'This island's mine' make the claim blunt and firm, and the **present tense** 'tak'st' suggests the theft is still going on. He remembers a time of kindness: Prospero 'strok'st me and made much of me', gave him 'Water with berries in't' and taught him to name 'the bigger light, and how the less' (the sun and moon). In return, Caliban 'show'd thee all the qualities o' th' isle'. The list 'The fresh springs, brine-pits, barren place and fertile' shows his deep knowledge of the land — the knowledge that let the newcomers survive. Now he is penned up: 'here you sty me / In this hard rock'. The verb **'sty'** compares him to a pig in a pen, showing he is treated as an animal.\n\nAnswering Miranda, who calls him 'Abhorred slave' and reminds him that she 'Took pains to make thee speak' (the First Folio gives this speech to Miranda, though some editions reassign it to Prospero), his most famous retort turns his masters' 'gift' against them: 'You taught me language; and my profit on't / Is, I know how to curse.' The language he was taught has become the weapon he fights back with.\n\n**The case against him.** Prospero says he treated Caliban 'with human care' and lodged him in his own home until Caliban tried to attack Miranda. Caliban does not deny it — he says he wishes he had succeeded, so that he could have 'peopled… / This isle with Calibans'. Later he plots Prospero's murder and swaps one master for another, kneeling to the drunken Stephano. Prospero calls him 'A devil, a born devil, on whose nature / Nurture can never stick' (4.1).\n\n**Nature vs nurture.** This is the debate about whether character comes from what you are born with (**nature**) or how you are raised and educated (**nurture**). Prospero claims education ('nurture') failed with Caliban. But you could argue that Caliban's anger was *created* by how he was treated.\n\n**Colonial readings.** Since the twentieth century many readers and directors have seen Caliban as a **colonised** person: an inhabitant whose land is taken, who is given a new language and then enslaved and called a savage. Writers such as Aimé Césaire (in his 1969 play *Une Tempête*) rewrote the story from Caliban's side.\n\nAt the end, Prospero says 'this thing of darkness I / Acknowledge mine', and Caliban says he will 'seek for grace'. Whether this is a reconciliation or just a defeat is for you to argue.",
      passage: pCaliban,
      diagram:
        '<svg viewBox="0 0 340 200" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Connotation web for the word sty: pig pen, dirt and filth, treated as an animal not a human, locked in and confined"><line x1="170" y1="95" x2="70" y2="38" stroke="#be123c" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="170" y1="95" x2="270" y2="38" stroke="#be123c" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="170" y1="95" x2="70" y2="152" stroke="#be123c" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="170" y1="95" x2="270" y2="152" stroke="#be123c" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="170" cy="95" r="36" fill="#ffe4e6" stroke="#be123c" stroke-width="2"/><text x="170" y="92" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle" fill="#881337">sty</text><text x="170" y="110" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#881337">(verb)</text><rect x="12" y="22" width="116" height="32" rx="16" fill="#fff1f2" stroke="#fda4af"/><text x="70" y="42" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#881337">a pig pen</text><rect x="212" y="22" width="116" height="32" rx="16" fill="#fff1f2" stroke="#fda4af"/><text x="270" y="42" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#881337">dirt, filth</text><rect x="4" y="136" width="132" height="32" rx="16" fill="#fff1f2" stroke="#fda4af"/><text x="70" y="156" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#881337">animal, not human</text><rect x="212" y="136" width="116" height="32" rx="16" fill="#fff1f2" stroke="#fda4af"/><text x="270" y="156" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="#881337">locked in</text><text x="170" y="192" font-size="12" font-style="italic" text-anchor="middle" fill="#334155">‘here you sty me / In this hard rock’</text></svg>',
